#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:1
msgid "Feature Name: `project_goals_2026`"
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:2
msgid "Start Date: 2026-02-23"
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:3
msgid ""
"RFC PR: [rust-lang/rfcs#3935](https://github.com/rust-lang/rfcs/pull/3935)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:5
msgid "Summary"
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:7
msgid ""
"Establish the initial round of Rust Project Goals for 2026 along with a set "
"of current roadmaps, which describe multi-year development arcs."
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:9
msgid ""
"New Rust Project Goals may be added over the course of the year but only if "
"all required resources (champions, funding, etc) are already known."
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:11
msgid "Motivation"
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:13
msgid ""
"The 2026 goal slate consists of 66 project goals. In comparison to prior "
"rounds, we have changed to _annual_ goals rather than six-month goal "
"periods. Annuals goals give us more time to discuss and organize."
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:15
msgid "Why we set goals"
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:17
msgid "Goals serve multiple purposes."
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:19
msgid ""
"For would-be contributors, goals are Rust's front door. If you want to "
"improve Rust - whether that's a new language feature, better tooling, or "
"fixing a long-standing pain point - the goal process is how you turn that "
"idea into reality. When you propose a goal and teams accept it, you get more "
"than approval. You get a _champion_ from the relevant team who will mentor "
"you, help you navigate the project, and ensure your work gets the review "
"attention it needs. Goals are a contract: you commit to doing the work, the "
"project commits to supporting you."
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:21
msgid ""
"For users, goals serve as a roadmap, giving you an early picture of what "
"work we expect to get done this year."
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:23
msgid ""
"For Rust maintainers, goals help to surface interactions across teams. They "
"aid in coordination because people know what work others are aiming to do "
"and where they may need to offer support."
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:25
msgid "Goals are _proposed_ by contributors and _accepted_ by teams"
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:27
msgid ""
"As an open-source project, Rust's goal process works differently than a "
"company's. In a company, leadership sets goals and assigns employees to "
"execute them. Rust doesn't have employees - we have contributors who "
"volunteer their time and energy. So in our process, goals begin with the "
"_contributor_: the person (or company) that wants to do the work."
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:29
msgid ""
"Contributors _propose_ goals; Rust teams _accept_ them. When you propose a "
"goal, you're saying you're prepared to invest the time to make it happen. "
"When a team accepts, they're committing to support that work - doing "
"reviews, engaging in RFC discussions, and providing the guidance needed to "
"land it in Rust."
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:31
msgid "How these goals were selected"
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:33
msgid ""
"Goal proposals were collected during the month of January. Many of the goals "
"are continuing goals that are carried over from the previous year, but "
"others goal are new."
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:35
msgid ""
"In February, an _alpha_ version of this RFC is reviewed with teams. Teams "
"vet the goals to determine if they are realistic and to make sure that goal "
"have champions from the team. A _champion_ is a Rust team member that will "
"mentor and guide the contributor as they do their work. Champions keep up "
"with progress on the goal, help the champion figure out technical "
"challenges, and also help the contributor to navigate the Rust team(s). "
"Champions also field questions from others in the project who want to "
"understand the goal."
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:37
msgid "How to follow along with a goal's progress"
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:39
msgid ""
"Once the Goals RFC is accepted, you can follow along with the progress on a "
"goal in a few different ways:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:41
msgid ""
"Each goal has a tracking issue. Goal contributors and champions are expected "
"to post regular updates. These updates are also posted to Zulip in the "
"`#project-goals` channel."
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:42
msgid "Regular blog posts cover major happenings in goals."
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:44
msgid "Guide-level explanation"
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:46
msgid ""
"There are a total of 66 planned for this year. That's a lot! You can see the "
"complete list, but to help you get a handle on it, we've selected a few to "
"highlight. These are goals that will be stabilizing this year or which we "
"think people will be particularly excited to learn about."
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:48
msgid ""
"**Important:** You have to understand the nature of a Rust goal. Rust is an "
"open-source project, which means that progress only happens when "
"contributors come and _make_ it happen. When the Rust project declares a "
"goal, that means that (a) contributors, who we call the _task owners_, have "
"said they want to do the work and (b) members of the Rust team members have "
"promised to support them. Sometimes those task owners are volunteers, "
"sometimes they are paid by a company, and sometimes they supported by "
"grants. But no matter which category they are, if they ultimately are not "
"able to do the work (say, because something else comes up that is higher "
"priority for them in their lives), then the goal won't happen. That's ok, "
"there's always next year!"
msgstr ""

