#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:54
msgid ""
"Stabilize support for \"cargo script\", the ability to have a single file "
"that contains both Rust code and a `Cargo.toml`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:56
msgid "_People involved: [Ed Page](https://github.com/epage)_"
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:60
msgid ""
"\"Cargo script\" let's you create a single file that specifies both a Rust "
"program and the dependencies it needs and then execute that program with one "
"convenient command. For example, you can now take a Rust file like this:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:67
msgid "\"0.12\""
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:67
msgid "\"blocking\""
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:71
msgid "\"https://httpbin.org/ip\""
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:75
msgid "\"My IP info: {body}\""
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:79
msgid ""
"and run it with `cargo my_ip.rs`.  Or, thanks to the `#!` line, you can just "
"run `./my_ip.rs`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:81
msgid ""
"This feature makes good use of [one of the things we found when doing our "
"research for the Vision Doc](https://blog.rust-lang.org/2025/12/19/what-do-"
"people-love-about-rust/#but-what-they-love-is-the-sense-of-empowerment-and-"
"versatility): that people love Rust not only because it helps them build "
"foundational software, but because it's a expressive and productive enough "
"that \"you can write everything from the top to the bottom of your stack in "
"it\" (-- Rust expert and consultant focused on embedded and real-time "
"systems). Until now, the fly in the ointment was that packaging up a Rust "
"package required several files and required people to do a separate "
"compilation step. Cargo script solves that problem."
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:83
msgid "The borrow checker will be more flexible with _Polonius alpha_"
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:87
msgid ""
"[Stabilize polonius alpha](https://rust-lang.github.io/rust-project-"
"goals/2026/polonius.html#stabilize-polonius-alpha)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:87
msgid ""
"Fix remaining issues, validate on real-world code, and ship a stable "
"improved borrow checker."
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:88
msgid ""
"[Extend formality for Polonius alpha](https://rust-lang.github.io/rust-"
"project-goals/2026/polonius.html#extend-formality-for-polonius-alpha)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:88
msgid ""
"Build a formal model of borrow checking in a-mir-formality and upstream it "
"into the Rust reference."
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:90
msgid ""
"_People involved: [Amanda Stjerna](https://github.com/amandasystems), [Jack "
"Huey](https://github.com/jackh726), [Rémy Rakic](https://github.com/lqd), "
"[Niko Matsakis](https://github.com/nikomatsakis), [tiif](https://github.com/"
"tiif)_"
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:94
msgid ""
"The \"Polonius Alpha\" work represents the final completion of the original "
"promise from the [2018 Non-lexical Lifetimes RFC](https://rust-lang.github."
"io/rfcs/2094-nll.html). That RFC originally planned to address three "
"problematic patterns -- but ultimately, for efficiency reasons, we were only "
"able to fix two. In the meantime, for the last several years, we have been "
"pursuing work on Polonius, a next generation borrow checker formulation, "
"that aims to close this gap and more."
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:96
msgid ""
"The Polonius Alpha rules extend the borrow checker to accept the so-called "
"[\"Problem Case #3\"](https://rust-lang.github.io/rfcs/2094-nll.html#problem-"
"case-3-conditional-control-flow-across-functions) that NLL ultimately failed "
"to solve. This case occurs when you have conditional control flow across "
"functions. For example, in this case the call to `map.get_mut(&key)`, the "
"borrow of `map` is only \"live\" in the `Some` branch, where it is returned "
"(and hence must outlive `'r`). But because of imprecision in the borrow "
"checker, the borrow winds up being enforced in the `None` branch as well, "
"resulting in an error:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:103
msgid "// ──────────────────┐ 'r only needs to\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:104
msgid "// ◄────┘ be valid here...\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:105 src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:108
#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:109
msgid "//      │\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:106
msgid ""
"//      │\n"
"            //  ^~~~~~ ERROR               //      │\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:110
msgid "//      │ ...but today it covers\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:111
msgid "// ◄────┘ all this\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:114
msgid "Under Polonius Alpha, this code compiles."
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:116
msgid ""
"Polonius Alpha is part of a larger roadmap called [the Borrow-Checker Within]"
"(https://rust-lang.github.io/rust-project-goals/2026/roadmap-borrow-checker-"
"within.html) that we expect to be driving over the next few years. This "
"year, another part of that work is including Polonius Alpha in [a-mir-"
"formality](https://github.com/rust-lang/a-mir-formality/), the [types team's]"
"(https://rust-lang.org/governance/teams/compiler/#team-types) (in-progress) "
"specification for how the Rust type system works. As part of another goal, "
"we are planning to [integrate a-mir-formality into the Rust reference]"
"(https://rust-lang.github.io/rust-project-goals/2026/a-mir-formality.html). "
"This would make Polonius the first version of the borrow checker whose "
"behavior is specified outside of the Rust compiler."
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:118
msgid ""
"Change const evaluation to support _traits_, and _reflection_, allow "
"_structs/enums_ as const parameter types"
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:122
msgid ""
"[ADT const params](https://rust-lang.github.io/rust-project-goals/2026/const-"
"generics.html#adt-const-params)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:122
msgid "Support structs, tuples, arrays in const generics."
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:123
msgid ""
"[Min generic const arguments](https://rust-lang.github.io/rust-project-"
"goals/2026/const-generics.html#min-generic-const-arguments)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:123
msgid ""
"Support associated constants and generic parameters embedded in other "
"expressions."
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:124
msgid ""
"[Stabilize const traits MVP](https://rust-lang.github.io/rust-project-"
"goals/2026/const-traits.html#stabilize-const-traits-mvp)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:124
msgid ""
"Finalize the RFC, complete the compiler implementation, and stabilize so "
"`const fn` can call trait methods."
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:125
msgid ""
"[Explore design space for comptime `const fn`](https://rust-lang.github.io/"
"rust-project-goals/2026/reflection-and-comptime.html#explore-design-space-"
"for-comptime-const-fn)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:125
msgid ""
"Implement and validate `#[compile_time_only]` attribute for `const fn` that "
"enables type reflection without runtime overhead."
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:127
msgid ""
"_People involved: [Boxy](https://github.com/BoxyUwU), [Deadbeef](https://"
"github.com/fee1-dead), [Josh Triplett](https://github.com/joshtriplett), "
"[Niko Matsakis](https://github.com/nikomatsakis), [Oliver Scherer](https://"
"github.com/oli-obk), [Scott McMurray](https://github.com/scottmcm), [TC]"
"(https://github.com/traviscross)_"
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:131
msgid ""
"This year we'll be extending Rust's support for const evaluation in several "
"ways. To start, you'll be able to use structs and enums as the values for "
"const generics, not only integers. So where today you can write `Array<3>`, "
"you'll be able to write something like this:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:140
msgid "// ...\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:148
msgid ""
"You'll also be able to use associated constants as const generic arguments, "
"like `Buffer<T::MAX_SIZE>`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:150
msgid ""
"Next, we are integrating `const` into the trait system. When you implement a "
"trait, you'll be able to provide a `const` impl which means that the methods "
"in the trait are all const-compatible. `const fn` can then use bounds like "
"`T: const Display` to indicate that they need a type with a const-compatible "
"impl or `T: [const] Display` to indicate that they need a const-compatible "
"impl when called in a const context. Const traits are particularly helpful "
"because they allow you to use builtin language constructs like `?` and `for` "
"loops:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:162
msgid ""
"Finally, we're beginning early experimental work on compile-time reflection "
"— the ability for const functions to inspect type information. It's too "
"early to promise specifics, but the long-term vision is things like "
"serialization working without derive macros."
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:164
msgid "Ergonomic ref-counting and (maybe) async traits"
msgstr ""

#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:168 src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:384
#: src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:534 src/3935-Project-Goals-2026.md:572
